    "FOLLOW-UP": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "PATIENT MONITORING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "canonical_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Care must be taken to avoid insulin overdose causing hypoglycemia during the immediate postpartum period (or the end of diestrus), because the speed at which insulin resistance resolves and exogenous insulin requirements decrease is unpredictable.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE",
          "canonical_heading": "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ovariohysterectomy to remove source of progesterone.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Lack of prompt resolution of hyperglycemia may result in diabetes mellitus becoming permanent.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS.",
          "canonical_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diabetes usually resolves at parturition or at the end of diestrus. More likely to be transient DM if pregnancy terminated, whereas insulin-treated bitches more likely to develop permanent DM.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May abort the litter or have dystocia as a result of the effects of chronic hyperglycemia.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Small, unthrifty puppies may result from an abnormal placental blood supply. Conversely, some fetuses in a hyperglycemic environment experience an abnormally increased growth",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "rate (macrosomia). These puppies tend to be large, leading to dystocia.",
              "pdf_page": 643,
              "printed_page": 561,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
